<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dillingen an der Donau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dillingen an der Donau is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia-bavaria">Bavarian Swabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The seat of the counts of Dillingen, it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in 1258 and became their residence after they left their cathedral city. Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-truchsess-von-waldburg">Otto Truchsess von Waldburg</a> founded its university in 1551, run by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> from 1563, which made the town a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dillingen-an-der-donau.jpg" alt="Dillingen an der Donau" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jkü · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%B6nigsstra%C3%9Fe_Dillingen.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
